      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve for \\(\\angle CED\\), we start by analyzing the given geometric configuration. The circle is centered at \\(O\\) with \\(AB\\) as the diameter. This means that \\(\\angle AEB = 90^\\circ\\) because it is an inscribed angle that subtends the semicircle \\(AB\\).\n\nWe are given that \\(\\angle BAE = 43^\\circ\\). Since \\(AB\\) is the diameter, \\(\\angle AEB = 90^\\circ\\). Therefore, in triangle \\(ABE\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\angle ABE = 90^\\circ - \\angle BAE = 90^\\circ - 43^\\circ = 47^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find \\(\\angle CED\\). Since \\(BC\\) and \\(BE\\) are tangents to the circle at points \\(B\\) and \\(E\\) respectively, \\(\\angle OBE = 90^\\circ\\). This makes quadrilateral \\(OBEC\\) cyclic with \\(\\angle OBE = 90^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle OEC = 90^\\circ\\).\n\nNow, consider triangle \\(CDE\\). We need to find \\(\\angle CED\\). Notice that \\(\\angle OBE = 90^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle OEC = 90^\\circ\\), which imply that \\(O\\) is the point where the tangents from \\(E\\) and \\(B\\) are equal in length. This means that the angle \\(\\angle CED\\) is the same as the angle \\(\\angle OCE\\), which is \\(90^\\circ\\).\n\nHowever, we need to find \\(\\angle CED\\) in the context of the circle and the given angles. Since \\(\\angle BAE = 43^\\circ\\), and knowing that \\(\\angle BAE\\) is an inscribed angle that subtends the arc \\(BE\\), the angle \\(\\angle CED\\) is the same as \\(\\angle BAE\\) because they are corresponding angles in the cyclic quadrilateral \\(OEBD\\).\n\nTherefore, \\(\\angle CED = \\angle BAE = 43^\\circ\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{43}\n\\]",
