      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find \\(\\angle CED\\), we start by analyzing the given information and the properties of the circle and the tangents.\n\n1. **Identify Key Angles and Properties:**\n   - \\(AB\\) is the diameter of the circle, so \\(\\angle AEB = 90^\\circ\\).\n   - \\(E\\) is a point on the circle, so \\(\\angle AEB = 90^\\circ\\) implies that \\(\\angle AEB = 90^\\circ\\).\n   - The tangent at \\(B\\) intersects the tangent at \\(E\\) and \\(AE\\) at \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) respectively.\n\n2. **Determine \\(\\angle BAE\\):**\n   - Given \\(\\angle BAE = 43^\\circ\\).\n\n3. **Find \\(\\angle ABE\\):**\n   - Since \\(B\\) and \\(E\\) are endpoints of the diameter \\(AB\\), the angle subtended by the diameter is a right angle.\n   - Therefore, \\(\\angle ABE = 90^\\circ - \\angle BAE = 90^\\circ - 43^\\circ = 47^\\circ\\).\n\n4. **Use the Tangent-Secant Property:**\n   - The tangent at \\(B\\) intersects the tangent at \\(E\\) and \\(AE\\) at \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) respectively.\n   - The angles formed by the tangents and the chord are related to the central angles they subtend.\n\n5. **Find \\(\\angle CED\\):**\n   - Since \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are points where the tangents at \\(B\\) and \\(E\\) intersect, and \\(AE\\) is a chord, \\(\\angle CED\\) can be found using the fact that the angle between the tangents is equal to the angle subtended by the arc \\(CE\\) at the center of the circle.\n   - The angle \\(\\angle COE\\) is twice the angle \\(\\angle CED\\) because \\(\\angle COE\\) is the central angle subtended by the same arc as \\(\\angle CED\\) in the cyclic quadrilateral \\(OBCED\\).\n\n6. **Calculate \\(\\angle COE\\):**\n   - Since \\(\\angle AEB = 90^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle BAE = 43^\\circ\\), the angle \\(\\angle COE\\) is:\n     \\[\n     \\angle COE = 2 \\times \\angle COB = 2 \\times (90^\\circ - \\angle BAE) = 2 \\times (90^\\circ - 43^\\circ) = 2 \\times 47^\\circ = 94^\\circ\n     \\]\n\n7. **Determine \\(\\angle CED\\):**\n   - The angle \\(\\angle CED\\) is half of \\(\\angle COE\\) because \\(\\angle CED\\) is an inscribed angle subtending the same arc as \\(\\angle COE\\):\n     \\[\n     \\angle CED = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\angle COE = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 94^\\circ = 47^\\circ\n     \\]\n\nThus, the measure of \\(\\angle CED\\) is \\(\\boxed{47}\\).",
